WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.133 --> 00:00:02.234
-How you doing?
-I'm doing good.

00:00:02.234 --> 00:00:03.667
Great to see you.

00:00:03.667 --> 00:00:05.100
-Do they have April Fools' Day
in Australia, your homeland?

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:06.400
-We have it.
It's Australia, hello.

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:07.601
Yeah, like...
-Yeah.

00:00:07.601 --> 00:00:09.033
-We have it
for the entire month of April.

00:00:09.033 --> 00:00:10.734
Actually, I do remember --

00:00:10.734 --> 00:00:14.000
I remember one time my dad
freaking out on April Fools'

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:16.067
because in
"The Sydney Morning Herald,"

00:00:16.067 --> 00:00:17.400
Volvo -- we had a Volvo.

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:18.968
Do you remember the old Volvo --
they used to call them

00:00:18.968 --> 00:00:20.200
the fridge?
-Yeah.

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:21.501
-You know, they're like
a fridge turned over.

00:00:21.501 --> 00:00:23.701
So, we had a --
we had a fridge Volvo.

00:00:23.701 --> 00:00:25.868
And they had a full-page ad

00:00:25.868 --> 00:00:28.367
announcing that
there were fake Volvos out there

00:00:28.367 --> 00:00:30.000
and you could tell
by the emblem --

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:31.934
whatever the emblem
of Volvo was,

00:00:31.934 --> 00:00:33.234
it was flipped the wrong way.

00:00:33.234 --> 00:00:36.067
So my dad's, like,
running out and freaking out.

00:00:36.067 --> 00:00:38.634
And then when we found out
this was a full-page

00:00:38.634 --> 00:00:39.934
April Fools' joke.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:39.934 --> 00:00:41.934
Most people would be like,
"That's brilliant."

00:00:41.934 --> 00:00:43.667
But my dad was like,
"Oh, curses!"

00:00:43.667 --> 00:00:45.200
Like...
[ Laughter ]

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:47.834
-Was your dad not
a fooling-around guy?

00:00:47.834 --> 00:00:49.534
-He was just always late, rushed
-- No.

00:00:49.534 --> 00:00:51.067
We used to watch
"Fawlty Towers" together,

00:00:51.067 --> 00:00:52.300
and he couldn't laugh.

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:53.534
He goes, "This is just too close
to the bone."

00:00:53.534 --> 00:00:54.601
Like, he would get too...

00:00:54.601 --> 00:00:55.634
-Really?
-...freaked out.

00:00:55.634 --> 00:00:57.300
Yeah.
-Wow, you really, rebelled,

00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:58.634
I guess,
against that, didn't you?

00:00:58.634 --> 00:00:59.801
-A little. Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:00:59.801 --> 00:01:01.000
-A little.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:02.200
-And thanks
for keeping this a secret.

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:03.267
You've known about this
for quite some time.

00:01:03.267 --> 00:01:04.434
-I have, the whole time.

00:01:04.434 --> 00:01:05.667
-Are you good at keeping
secrets in general?

00:01:05.667 --> 00:01:06.934
-Terrible, the worst.
-Oh, you're not?

00:01:06.934 --> 00:01:08.934
-I'm the worst.
I'm a bad liar.

00:01:08.934 --> 00:01:11.033
I mean, for an actor,
I make a living out of lying.

00:01:11.033 --> 00:01:12.133
-Yeah.
-I'm really, really bad at it.

00:01:12.133 --> 00:01:13.300
[ Laughter ]

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:15.701
The worst time I had to lie,
actually,

00:01:15.701 --> 00:01:17.801
was when I got
the role of Wolverine.

00:01:17.801 --> 00:01:21.734
So this was --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:21.734 --> 00:01:24.200
[ Laughter ]

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:25.467
Shameless fishing.

00:01:25.467 --> 00:01:27.801
No, no, no.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:27.801 --> 00:01:28.901
I went to Toronto.

00:01:28.901 --> 00:01:30.200
I auditioned.
I got the part.

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:33.300
I had to go back to L.A.
to pick up my stuff.

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:35.067
And they said,
"We need you there tomorrow."

00:01:35.067 --> 00:01:36.167
They had started shooting.

00:01:36.167 --> 00:01:37.567
So I went the next day,
and they said,

00:01:37.567 --> 00:01:39.868
"By the way,
when you go back into customs,

00:01:39.868 --> 00:01:42.501
just say that you're there
for an audition."

00:01:42.501 --> 00:01:45.434
And I said, "Okay, but I have
got the part, right?

00:01:45.434 --> 00:01:46.901
Because I just auditioned."

00:01:46.901 --> 00:01:48.434
They said, "No, no, since
we haven't gotten your visa --

00:01:48.434 --> 00:01:50.300
You're Australian.
The visa hasn't come in time.

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:51.801
It'll be fine.

00:01:51.801 --> 00:01:53.200
Just say it's an audition,
and then we'll sort it out."

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:54.901
[ Laughter ]
So, this is like

00:01:54.901 --> 00:01:56.033
"Midnight Express" for me.

00:01:56.033 --> 00:01:57.534
I'm in the line.
-Uh-huh.

00:01:57.534 --> 00:01:58.868
-"It's an audition.

00:01:58.868 --> 00:01:59.868
This is just an audition.
This is just an audition."

00:01:59.868 --> 00:02:01.100
I'm sweating, I'm sweating.

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:02.501
[ Laughter ]
The guy -- I'm looking.

00:02:02.501 --> 00:02:03.767
The guy looks kind of nerdy.

00:02:03.767 --> 00:02:05.200
I think, "Ah, I'm alright."

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:06.868
And I get up there, and he goes,
"Hey, what are you here for?"

00:02:06.868 --> 00:02:08.534
And I said,
"Oh, just doing an audition."

00:02:08.534 --> 00:02:10.267
And he goes, "Really?"

00:02:10.267 --> 00:02:11.534
I said, "Yeah."

00:02:11.534 --> 00:02:12.667
And he goes,
"Well, that's funny,

00:02:12.667 --> 00:02:14.033
because you seemed
to be here last week.

00:02:14.033 --> 00:02:15.834
What were you here
last week for?"

00:02:15.834 --> 00:02:16.901
[ Light laughter ]
And I'm like --

00:02:16.901 --> 00:02:18.834
sweat just pouring down.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:18.834 --> 00:02:20.968
And I --
I literally then said,

00:02:20.968 --> 00:02:23.167
"No, no, it's not an audition.
I've got the part."

00:02:23.167 --> 00:02:25.667
I just broke.
It was pathetic.

00:02:25.667 --> 00:02:27.467
[ Laughter ]
And he goes, "Go over there."

00:02:27.467 --> 00:02:28.901
And it was that room --
you know, like, the room.

00:02:28.901 --> 00:02:30.133
-I've been in that room.
-Ohh.

00:02:30.133 --> 00:02:31.300
You don't want to be
in that room.

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:32.300
-Yeah, I've been
in that room, yeah.

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:33.534
-That room is bad.
-Yeah.

00:02:33.534 --> 00:02:34.534
-And I went in there,
and I was there for

00:02:34.534 --> 00:02:35.801
like an hour and a half.

00:02:35.801 --> 00:02:37.267
And he made me wait.
And I went to make a phone call.

00:02:37.267 --> 00:02:40.801
That guy literally is like,
"Get off the phone now!"

00:02:40.801 --> 00:02:42.400
-Really?
-"This is serious!

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:45.501
You have no rights in here!
You have no phone calls!"

00:02:45.501 --> 00:02:47.501
And I'm sitting there going,
"Okay, first of all,

00:02:47.501 --> 00:02:49.734
I've just lost
the biggest break of my life."

00:02:49.734 --> 00:02:50.934
[ Laughter ]
"I'm going to go to jail,

00:02:50.934 --> 00:02:52.767
and I don't know what to do
and..."

00:02:52.767 --> 00:02:55.701
-And you met
the first mean Canadian ever.

00:02:55.701 --> 00:02:57.234
-Ever, yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:57.234 --> 00:02:59.701
So I finally get up there,
and he's like,

00:02:59.701 --> 00:03:02.434
"Listen, we're probably going to
send you back on a plane."

00:03:02.434 --> 00:03:04.334
What was --
And I said, "Listen, I'm sorry.

00:03:04.334 --> 00:03:06.300
It's just -- I've got this part
in this thing,

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:07.467
and they haven't got my visa."

00:03:07.467 --> 00:03:08.734
And he goes, "What's the thing?"

00:03:08.734 --> 00:03:11.934
And I said, "Well, it's 'X-Men.'
It's like a comic book."

00:03:11.934 --> 00:03:13.834
He goes, "What?"
[ Laughter ]

00:03:13.834 --> 00:03:15.133
I said -- he goes,

00:03:15.133 --> 00:03:17.534
"Well, you mean like
the animation thing, 'X-Men'?"

00:03:17.534 --> 00:03:18.801
And I goes, "Well, no,

00:03:18.801 --> 00:03:19.934
they're doing
a live-action version of it."

00:03:19.934 --> 00:03:21.434
He goes, "What?"
[ Laughter ]

00:03:21.434 --> 00:03:22.701
And I said, "Yeah."

00:03:22.701 --> 00:03:24.601
And he goes, "Well,
what did you audition for?"

00:03:24.601 --> 00:03:27.133
And I said, "It's a character
called Wolverine."

00:03:27.133 --> 00:03:29.734
He goes, "What?!"
[ Laughter ]

00:03:29.734 --> 00:03:31.501
And he literally looked at me,
and then he just --

00:03:31.501 --> 00:03:32.767
to all his mates, he goes,

00:03:32.767 --> 00:03:37.000
"Hey, Jimmy, it's Wolverine!
This is Wolverine!"

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:38.767
[ Laughter ]
Honestly, I went out there

00:03:38.767 --> 00:03:40.367
with a police escort,
just to let me out.

00:03:40.367 --> 00:03:43.200
This guy made me
sign autographs, everything.

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:44.534
[ Laughter ]
-Wolverine's --

00:03:44.534 --> 00:03:46.067
-He was a mad "X-Men" fan.

00:03:46.067 --> 00:03:47.801
And that's the only reason
I ended up getting the part.

00:03:47.801 --> 00:03:49.033
-The biggest
Canadian superhero, too.

00:03:49.033 --> 00:03:50.634
-Yeah.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:50.634 --> 00:03:54.467
-Wow. Wow. Well, I'm glad.
Had I known any of that stuff,

00:03:54.467 --> 00:03:56.167
I never would have asked you
to be here tonight.

00:03:56.167 --> 00:03:57.667
But I do appreciate it.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:57.667 --> 00:03:59.000
-I did get strip-searched
before that.

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:00.601
But we won't get into that.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:00.601 --> 00:04:04.434
-We --We --
Actually, you are doing --

00:04:04.434 --> 00:04:06.467
what -- eight shows a week now?
-Eight shows a week, yeah.

00:04:06.467 --> 00:04:07.601
-And so this makes it nine.

00:04:07.601 --> 00:04:08.868
Thank you for doing it.
-My pleasure.

00:04:08.868 --> 00:04:10.267
-I complain about doing
four shows a week.

00:04:10.267 --> 00:04:12.300
Eight shows a week
is a lot of shows a week.

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:15.334
-Yeah, but I'm, honestly, Jimmy,
having the time of my life.

00:04:15.334 --> 00:04:17.801
-It's like the biggest --
it's more -- it's like

00:04:17.801 --> 00:04:20.868
bigger than "Hamilton"
and -- I don't know -- "Cats"

00:04:20.868 --> 00:04:23.300
or whatever's going on
out there, all combined, right?

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:24.934
It's a huge thing.
-[ Chuckling ] Yes.

00:04:24.934 --> 00:04:27.367
-It's like -- It's really --

00:04:27.367 --> 00:04:28.834
-Listen, we're just having
the best time.

00:04:28.834 --> 00:04:30.300
The theatre,
the Winter Garden Theatre,

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:31.601
is just filled with joy.

00:04:31.601 --> 00:04:34.934
You feel the audience --
"A," the show is full of joy,

00:04:34.934 --> 00:04:37.100
but you just feel them being
happy to be back.

00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:39.133
Oh, my God.
-It's also filled

00:04:39.133 --> 00:04:41.267
with some items that...
-Oh, my God. Yeah.

00:04:41.267 --> 00:04:42.567
-...were gifted to you
by a fellow actor.

00:04:42.567 --> 00:04:46.734
-Yeah. Yeah, opening-night gifts
are big on Broadway.

00:04:46.734 --> 00:04:49.067
And so you walk in --
there's flowers, there's cards,

00:04:49.067 --> 00:04:51.567
and, of course,
right in my makeup,

00:04:51.567 --> 00:04:54.100
where I put my makeup
in the mirror, there was this.

00:04:54.100 --> 00:04:55.467
Thank you.

00:04:55.467 --> 00:04:57.367
-This photograph
sent to you by...?

00:04:57.367 --> 00:04:58.367
-Ryan Reynolds.

00:04:58.467 --> 00:04:59.501
-Ryan Reynolds.

00:04:59.501 --> 00:05:00.834
[ Laughter ]

00:05:00.834 --> 00:05:03.634
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:03.634 --> 00:05:05.634
With a note.
[ Laughs ]

00:05:05.634 --> 00:05:07.868
-A little note.

00:05:07.868 --> 00:05:09.601
-He'll be --
"I'll be watching."

00:05:09.601 --> 00:05:10.701
Somewhat ominous.

00:05:10.701 --> 00:05:14.033
-So, I was taking that in,
and I thought --

00:05:14.033 --> 00:05:15.467
I got up from my seat.
I was laughing.

00:05:15.467 --> 00:05:16.767
And I went over to brush
my teeth.

00:05:16.767 --> 00:05:19.133
Behind me -- there's another
mirror behind me, and...

00:05:19.133 --> 00:05:21.167
Thank you, Ryan Reynolds.
-And he sent you this, too.

00:05:21.167 --> 00:05:22.300
-There was that.
-There's this.

00:05:22.300 --> 00:05:23.634
[ Cheers and applause ]
-So...

00:05:23.634 --> 00:05:27.334
-Oh. Is that an original?
[ Laughter ]

00:05:27.334 --> 00:05:28.801
-I just --

00:05:28.801 --> 00:05:30.367
He follows me wherever I go!

00:05:30.367 --> 00:05:32.133
-This should be hanging
in a museum in Halifax

00:05:32.133 --> 00:05:33.300
or something.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:35.801
That is quite --
well, that is quite beautiful.

00:05:35.801 --> 00:05:38.367
-I am working with, I mean --
Sutton Foster, I think,

00:05:38.367 --> 00:05:40.968
is the greatest in the world
at what she does,

00:05:40.968 --> 00:05:42.601
which is seemingly everything.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:42.601 --> 00:05:44.234
-I agree.
I totally agree.

00:05:44.234 --> 00:05:46.501
-Jefferson Mays,
Jayne Houdyshell,

00:05:46.501 --> 00:05:48.567
Shuler Hensley, Marie Mullen.

00:05:48.567 --> 00:05:49.901
It is --
It's like eight times a week --

00:05:49.901 --> 00:05:52.534
-Did you say "Sherman Hemsley"?
-Shuler Hensley.

00:05:52.534 --> 00:05:53.868
-Oh, okay.
I was just like, "Wow."

00:05:53.868 --> 00:05:56.767
-By the way, 6'6",
and he will kill you, so...

00:05:56.767 --> 00:05:58.367
[ Laughter ]
But it's like going --

00:05:58.367 --> 00:06:01.067
every night,
I go to, like, an all-star game

00:06:01.067 --> 00:06:02.334
where people
are actually trying.

00:06:02.334 --> 00:06:03.367
It's really incredible.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:03.367 --> 00:06:04.634
-Yeah.
-You know?

00:06:04.634 --> 00:06:06.701
-You have almost too much
talent, I feel like.

00:06:06.701 --> 00:06:07.901
I feel like some of your talent

00:06:07.901 --> 00:06:10.200
should be spread around
to others, you know?

00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:11.734
-Is Ryan Reynolds
watching this, by the way?

00:06:11.734 --> 00:06:13.267
[ Laughter ]
Just a little check.

00:06:13.267 --> 00:06:14.634
-If he hears
his picture's on it,

00:06:14.634 --> 00:06:16.701
he'll be watching --
that's for sure.

00:06:16.701 --> 00:06:17.734
[ Laughter ]
-But, no, I'm loving it.

00:06:17.734 --> 00:06:18.901
My energy feels good.

00:06:18.901 --> 00:06:20.434
-Does it? 'Cause --
-Yeah, no, I do.

00:06:20.434 --> 00:06:22.701
I don't know why I haven't done
a full musical for 18 years.

00:06:22.701 --> 00:06:24.334
And everyone was like --
even Deb was like,

00:06:24.334 --> 00:06:25.534
"Babe, are you
going to be okay?"

00:06:25.534 --> 00:06:27.400
And I said, "What?
I'm going to be fine."

00:06:27.400 --> 00:06:29.868
There's only one thing where
I'm struggling a little bit...

00:06:29.868 --> 00:06:31.000
-What is that?

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:32.300
-...where, given my age,
is my bladder.

00:06:32.300 --> 00:06:33.434
I'm going to be --
I'm just going to be honest.

00:06:33.434 --> 00:06:35.300
[ Laughter ]
-Oh. Is it overactive?

00:06:35.300 --> 00:06:36.734
-Apparently.
-Ask your doctor --

00:06:36.734 --> 00:06:38.367
-I mean, not right now,
don't worry.

00:06:38.367 --> 00:06:40.467
Why? You don't care,
this is Jimmy Fallon's.

00:06:40.467 --> 00:06:42.033
[ Laughter ]

00:06:42.033 --> 00:06:44.167
The opening scene --
[ Applause ]

00:06:44.167 --> 00:06:46.767
So, let me give you
a little bit of context.

00:06:46.767 --> 00:06:48.634
'Cause this is
a three-hour show, right?

00:06:48.634 --> 00:06:49.868
Sorry.
-[ Laughs ] Sure.

00:06:49.868 --> 00:06:53.767
-I did once pee my pants onstage
when I was 26.

00:06:53.767 --> 00:06:55.467
You know, I was --
I drank too much water.

00:06:55.467 --> 00:06:57.133
Anyway, it's a long story.
Embarrassing.

00:06:57.133 --> 00:07:01.033
So, from that moment on,
I cannot hear the words,

00:07:01.033 --> 00:07:02.901
"Ladies and gentlemen,
this is your five-minute call,"

00:07:02.901 --> 00:07:04.567
without needing to go
to the bathroom, right?

00:07:04.567 --> 00:07:07.567
So I go religiously -- five
minutes before the show, I go.

00:07:07.567 --> 00:07:09.200
But the opening number of
"The Music Man,"

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:11.167
if you've ever seen it,
is on a train.

00:07:11.167 --> 00:07:12.567
I don't say a word,
I sit there.

00:07:12.567 --> 00:07:15.067
I hide behind a newspaper --
spoiler alert.

00:07:15.067 --> 00:07:19.434
I'm hiding behind it,
and literally bounce like this.

00:07:19.434 --> 00:07:21.601
-As if the train's going, yeah.
-For five minutes.

00:07:21.601 --> 00:07:24.000
And I start, and I'm like,
"Oh, I've got to go.

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:25.567
I've really got to go."
[ Laughter ]

00:07:25.567 --> 00:07:27.133
And when
that thought gets in there,

00:07:27.133 --> 00:07:28.534
it does not leave --
let me tell you.

00:07:28.534 --> 00:07:29.567
-I know. Yeah.

00:07:29.567 --> 00:07:30.934
-And the last thing
you want to do

00:07:30.934 --> 00:07:33.133
is a big song-and-dance number,
which is my first number,

00:07:33.133 --> 00:07:34.601
when you need to pee, so...
-Wow.

00:07:34.601 --> 00:07:35.934
-Anyway, so, for people
coming to the show,

00:07:35.934 --> 00:07:37.234
this could be
the scene to watch.

00:07:37.234 --> 00:07:39.434
-Wow, the stars really are
just like us, you know?

00:07:39.434 --> 00:07:41.267
[ Laughter ]

00:07:41.267 --> 00:07:43.000
Well, I know you have
to go do a show tonight.

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:44.501
Can you stick around
and play "Box of Lies"?

00:07:44.501 --> 00:07:45.767
-I'm in. Let's do it.
-Alright.

00:07:45.767 --> 00:07:46.901
Hugh Jackman is here.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:46.901 --> 00:07:48.467
"The Music Man"
is on Broadway now.

